 "council": {
  "title": "Clouds cost: sun optical-depth LOD range and a light-volume estimate",
  "question": "Is the near-sample six-step sun march what the Clouds pass spends its time on? Does moving its fade range closer, or replacing the crude far estimate by the cached light volume, save time or change the image?",
  "chosen": "baseline",
  "verdict": "No. Moving the sun-march fade range closer (near70: 20 to 70 instead of 45 to 140) or replacing the column estimate by the light-volume value (volume, volume70, volume40) leaves the Clouds pass at 8.68 to 9.12 ms against 8.68 ms in the first scene, and whole-frame times inside 0.3 to 1 ms, so the sun march is not where the time goes. The volume estimate also changes 25k to 50k pixels (the cached tau is not the column estimate it replaces), with no accuracy claim to show for it. Keep the shipped constants. The remaining Clouds cost is the march itself (steps, per-sample column and extinction lookups) and the per-pixel aerial perspective.",
  "lenses": [
   {
    "name": "GPU Engineer",
    "findings": "Clouds pass in sky_cumulus: baseline 8.68 ms, volume 8.68, near70 9.12, volume70 8.68, volume40 8.70 (one repeat launch 8.56). Cutting the exact sun march to a quarter of its range changed nothing, so it is not dominant. The per-pass numbers for cumulus_lowsun and orbit_deck in this run are not valid: the profiler panel froze on a hitch and returned the same snapshot twice (fixed in the harness afterwards: freeze_on_hitch off)."
   },
   {
    "name": "Minimalist / Anti-Overengineering Guardian",
    "findings": "Four variants, zero saving, visible pixel changes for the volume estimate: reject all and do not touch the sun march."
   },
